{
  "rationale": "The race evidence in entityR2A (iteration 1) points at dynamics and sensor span, not body width. Oval finishes 20 of 20. S-bend finishes 8 of 20, and every failure is off_track. Slalom fails in all 20 trials at progress 0.18. Max_error sits at 0.064 m on slalom and 0.051 m on s-bend, just past the 0.06 m floor. Steering oscillation (0.23 to 0.31) and line-loss events (0.8 to 2.0) rise sharply on the harder tracks, so the robot is turning too slowly to follow tight, alternating bends. Body width cannot help: the limit is max(0.06, 0.0125 + body_width/4), and it stays at the floor for any allowed width. Body length has no direct effect on the limit, but yaw inertia = m(L²+W²)/12 does affect how fast the robot can change heading. Turn rate is (wheel speed difference)/wheel_track, so a narrower track gives more yaw per unit of PWM difference.\n\nChanges I am making:\n1. Reduce yaw inertia: body_length 0.18 to 0.14 m and mass 0.5 to 0.45 kg. Inertia drops by about 25 percent, which should let the robot follow the slalom's rapid direction changes.\n2. Narrow wheel_track 0.12 to 0.11 m for more yaw authority per unit of wheel command. This is a moderate step to avoid overshoot.\n3. Widen the sensor span from ±0.024 to ±0.030 m with the same 16 mm footprints at 15 mm spacing. The outer sensors will see the tape sooner when the robot drifts toward the 0.06 m off-track limit, which should reduce the sudden line losses behind the off_track failures. Spacing of 15 mm is still under the 25 mm tape width, so the centre sensors keep a monotonic reading.\n4. Keep sensor y = 0.05 m, wheel_radius 0.03 m, and body_width 0.12 m. The y offset and wheel radius were not shown to be the limiting factor, and changing them now would confound the test.\n\nExpected result: slalom and s-bend progress should rise, with fewer off_track endings and a lower max_error. Oval should stay at finish rate 1.0. Because I change four things together, the Integrator's feedback on steering stability will show whether the inertia reduction or the wider sensor span is doing the work.",
  "geometry": {
    "body_length": 0.14,
    "body_width": 0.12,
    "mass": 0.45,
    "sensor_positions": [
      {"x": 0.03, "y": 0.05},
      {"x": 0.015, "y": 0.05},
      {"x": 0.0, "y": 0.05},
      {"x": -0.015, "y": 0.05},
      {"x": -0.03, "y": 0.05}
    ],
    "sensor_size": 0.016,
    "wheel_track": 0.11,
    "wheel_radius": 0.03
  },
  "intent": "Test whether lower yaw inertia (shorter body, lighter mass) and more turn authority per wheel command (narrower track) let the robot follow the tight bends on s-bend and slalom. Also widen the sensor span to ±0.03 m so drift toward the off-track limit is seen earlier. Expected: slalom progress above 0.18, fewer off_track endings, max_error below 0.06 m, and oval still finishing in every trial.",
  "feedback_request": {
    "questions": [
      "On slalom, does progress increase past 0.18, and is the failure still off_track at a specific bend? Which bend is it?",
      "Did the lower yaw inertia (body_length 0.14, mass 0.45) increase oscillation, or did steering_oscillation stay near 0.25 to 0.3?",
      "With sensors at ±0.03 m, does the signal stay monotonic across the tape, or do the outer sensors cause overshoot?",
      "Does wheel_track 0.11 m cause wheel saturation or slip at base_speed where the robot is fast?",
      "Which single change (inertia, track, or sensor span) do you think most affected the result, given your controller's behaviour?"
    ]
  },
  "learning": "Iteration 1 evidence: oval 20/20 finish, s-bend 8/20, slalom 0/20, all failures off_track, max_error 0.051 to 0.064 m. Hypotheses for this iteration: (H1) lower yaw inertia (body length and mass) lets the robot turn fast enough for slalom. (H2) narrower wheel track gives more turn per PWM difference, but may increase oscillation. (H3) ±0.03 m sensor span gives earlier warning of drift toward the 0.06 m limit. Check H1 through slalom progress and steering_oscillation, H2 through line_loss_events on s-bend, and H3 through max_error on all tracks.",
  "rationale_summary": "Reduce yaw inertia and widen the sensor span to address the bend-following failures seen in iteration 1."
}
